            <ArticleTitle>The Intestinal Immune System in Obesity and Insulin Resistance.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>413-26</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.cmet.2016.01.003</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S1550-4131(16)00037-1</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Obesity and insulin resistance are associated with chronic inflammation in metabolic tissues such as adipose tissue and the liver. Recently, growing evidence has implicated the intestinal immune system as an important contributor to metabolic disease. Obesity predisposes to altered intestinal immunity and is associated with changes to the gut microbiota, intestinal barrier function, gut-residing innate and adaptive immune cells, and oral tolerance to luminal antigens. Accordingly, the gut immune system may represent a novel therapeutic target for systemic inflammation in insulin resistance. This review discusses the emerging field of intestinal immunity in obesity-related insulin resistance and how it affects metabolic disease.</AbstractText>
